---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
---
<Base
  title="mhs — Active fractal"
  description="Quasi-static Coulomb loading of a synthetic 3-D fault network: 538 power-law faults from 2 to 38 km, 20771 mollified elements, and the subset that actually slips as the regional load rises."
  current="fractal"
>
  <Fragment slot="sidebar">
    <h1>Active fractal<span class="sub">which faults actually slip</span></h1>
    <p>
      A synthetic fault network with no slip prescribed anywhere. Each element
      carries a frictional strength, and slip is permitted only where the
      resolved stress reaches it. The regional load rises; faults activate,
      their stress changes load every other fault, and the network finds a new
      equilibrium.
    </p>
    <p>
      538 faults with <b>power-law lengths</b> from 2.0 to 37.9&nbsp;km in a
      100&nbsp;km box, 2 to 42&nbsp;km depth, 20,771&nbsp;elements of 1&nbsp;km
      edge, <i>&epsilon;</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;100&nbsp;m. Strike-slip regime,
      &mu;<sub>s</sub>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.6 with a 10&nbsp;% strength drop,
      confining stress 150&nbsp;MPa. The load is a <b>full stress tensor</b>
      on the path <i>k</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;2/3 &mdash; only
      <i>&sigma;<sub>H</sub></i> grows &mdash; so every element stays in
      compression at every frame.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <b>This is the experiment the on-fault interaction matrix exists for.</b>
      A stress-driven network needs <i>K<sub>ii</sub></i> &mdash; how much an
      element's own slip relieves its own shear &mdash; and for a classical
      dislocation element that number is undefined on the fault. Mollification
      makes it finite and, measured here, negative on every one of the 20,771
      elements.
    </div>
    <p class="contact">
      The slider is the <b>load parameter</b>, not time. Every frame is an
      equilibrium: all elements locked, every shear stress below its static
      strength. Nothing in this animation propagates.
    </p>
    <div class="links">
      <div><a href={`${base}concepts/`}>How the kernel works</a></div>
      <div><a href="https://github.com/brendanjmeade/mhs/tree/main/studies/active_fractal">The study code</a></div>
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <div id="viewer">
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="mode"><span>colour</span></label>
      <span data-ctl="play"></span>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="frame"><span>load</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="panel" data-iso-panel>
      <span data-ctl="iso"></span>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="thresh"><span>threshold</span></div>
    </div>
    <div data-canvas class="canvas" aria-label="A synthetic three-dimensional fault network of 538 power-law faults, coloured by which elements slip as the regional load increases"></div>
    <div data-bar class="bar"></div>
    <p data-status class="status">loading…</p>
  </div>

  <h2>What <i>&lambda;</i> is</h2>

  <p>
    The regional stress is a full tensor growing along a fixed path,
    <i>&sigma;</i>(<i>&lambda;</i>)&nbsp;=&nbsp;<i>&sigma;<sub>c</sub></i>&thinsp;<b>I</b>&nbsp;+&nbsp;<i>&lambda;</i>(<b>D</b>&nbsp;+&nbsp;<i>k</i>&thinsp;<b>I</b>).
    <b>D</b> is a fixed unit tensor giving the <i>shape</i> of the tectonic
    stress &mdash; its orientation and its faulting style &mdash;
    <i>&sigma;<sub>c</sub></i> is the confining pressure, here 150&nbsp;MPa,
    and <i>k</i> is the loading path. Only the magnitude grows during a run;
    the shape never changes, which is what lets a single number stand for the
    whole history.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>D</b>'s eigenvalues are (+1,&nbsp;0,&nbsp;&minus;1), so its maximum shear
    stress is exactly 1 and therefore <b><i>&lambda;</i> is the maximum shear
    stress of the tectonic field, in GPa</b> &mdash; equivalently half the
    differential stress, (<i>&sigma;</i><sub>1</sub>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;<i>&sigma;</i><sub>3</sub>)/2.
    The run starts at <i>&lambda;</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.1175, a tectonic field whose
    maximum shear stress is 118&nbsp;MPa, which is where the first element in
    this network reaches its failure criterion, and ends at 0.4525.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b><i>&lambda;</i> is not time.</b> It carries units of stress, nothing in
    the problem has a rate, and every frame is a static equilibrium. And
    <i>&lambda;</i> alone means nothing: the problem is invariant under scaling
    <i>&lambda;</i> and <i>&sigma;<sub>c</sub></i> together, so the physical
    parameter is the <i>ratio</i> of the two, never either by itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Not every fault can ever slip</h2>

  <p>
    The load clamps as well as shears, so a plane's strength grows with
    <i>&lambda;</i> too. Failure needs
    <i>&lambda;</i>|<i>&tau;</i>|&nbsp;&ge;&nbsp;<i>&mu;</i>(<i>&sigma;<sub>c</sub></i>&nbsp;+&nbsp;<i>&lambda;&sigma;<sub>n</sub></i>),
    so an element with
    |<i>&tau;</i>|&nbsp;&le;&nbsp;<i>&mu;&sigma;<sub>n</sub></i> per unit load
    <b>never fails at any load whatsoever</b>. On the path shipped here that is
    <b>28.3&nbsp;%</b> of elements, so the active set has a ceiling of
    71.7&nbsp;% rather than 100&nbsp;%. The gate checks this the blunt way: a
    locked element's yield function is still negative at
    <i>&lambda;</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;10<sup>8</sup>. Elastic interaction is almost
    powerless against it &mdash; on the purely deviatoric path it pushed
    exactly <b>two</b> of 2,116 locked elements over.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Measured rather than argued.</b> At <i>k</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;1, where the
    load has no ceiling and <i>&lambda;</i>&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;&infin; is a
    legitimate question, a run of 1,021,035 events out to 20&times; the first
    failure reaches 47.5&nbsp;% active against a predicted ceiling of
    50.5&nbsp;% &mdash; 94&nbsp;% of the way there, with the remainder
    arriving logarithmically. The active set overlaps the no-interaction
    prediction by 99.3&nbsp;%. So the ceiling is real, interaction does not
    lift it, and the answer to whether everything eventually slips is no by
    roughly half.
  </p>

  <p>
    The locked fraction is set by orientation, <i>&mu;</i> and the loading path
    alone, and it is <i>lumpy</i>, because fault lengths are power-law. Going
    from <i>&mu;</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.1 to 0.2 moves it from 0.17&nbsp;% to
    7.4&nbsp;%, and almost all of that jump is <b>one fault carrying 1,444
    elements</b> crossing the threshold. Counted in faults the curve is smooth
    (1, 3, 11, 32, 58, 142, 204, 257 of 538 as <i>&mu;</i> goes 0.05 to 1.0);
    counted in elements it is a staircase. A barrier here is one big
    badly-oriented fault, which is a consequence of the size distribution
    rather than of the mechanics.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the load is a full tensor and not a deviatoric one</h2>

  <div class="note">
    <b>An earlier version of this page was loaded past the bound below, and
    16.6&nbsp;% of its active set was failing for no mechanical reason.</b>
    The run shown now is compressive at every element in every frame, and the
    payload builder refuses to publish one that is not.
  </div>

  <p>
    A deviatoric <b>D</b> is traceless, so it raises the mean stress not at all:
    the tension-favoured planes shed normal stress exactly as fast as they gain
    shear. Past
    <i>&lambda;</i>&nbsp;&asymp;&nbsp;<i>&sigma;<sub>c</sub></i> their effective
    normal stress goes <i>negative</i>. Coulomb friction does not describe that
    &mdash; the fault opens &mdash; and worse, a negative
    <i>&sigma;</i><sub>eff</sub> makes the strength
    <i>&mu;&sigma;</i><sub>eff</sub> negative, so the element satisfies the
    yield criterion however little shear it carries and joins the active set
    for no mechanical reason.
  </p>

  <p>
    Purely deviatoric, the first element here opens at only 1.95&times; the
    first failure. Lowering <i>&sigma;<sub>c</sub></i> does not help, because
    first failure and opening scale with it identically &mdash; a lithostatic
    profile gives 2.15&times;. What helps is a loading path that also raises
    the mean stress, through the <i>k</i>&thinsp;<b>I</b> term:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th><i>k</i></th><th>the path</th><th>usable range</th>
      <th>locked</th><th>ceiling on active</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>0</td><td><i>&sigma;<sub>H</sub></i> up and
        <i>&sigma;<sub>h</sub></i> down equally; mean fixed</td>
        <td>1.95&times;</td><td>10.2&nbsp;%</td><td>89.8&nbsp;%</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>2/3</b></td><td><b>only <i>&sigma;<sub>H</sub></i> grows
        &mdash; shipped here</b></td><td><b>3.89&times;</b></td>
        <td><b>28.3&nbsp;%</b></td><td><b>71.7&nbsp;%</b></td></tr>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>minimum principal stress never falls</td>
        <td>unbounded</td><td>49.5&nbsp;%</td><td>50.5&nbsp;%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    <i>k</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;2/3 is not fitted to anything: holding
    <i>&sigma;<sub>h</sub></i> and <i>&sigma;<sub>v</sub></i> while
    <i>&sigma;<sub>H</sub></i> grows raises the mean stress at
    &Delta;/3 while the maximum shear rises at &Delta;/2. An isotropic tensor
    resolves the same normal stress onto every plane and no shear onto any, so
    <i>k</i> enters the arithmetic in exactly one place and every downstream
    formula is untouched &mdash; checked to machine precision rather than
    assumed.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>The more realistic the loading path, the fewer faults can ever
    slip.</b> That is the uncomfortable half of the table: going from a
    deviatoric load to one that never opens a fault cuts the ceiling from
    89.8&nbsp;% to 50.5&nbsp;%. The deviatoric case was flattering itself by
    driving a tenth of the network toward tension.
  </p>

  <p>
    None of the conclusions below depend on which of the three was used. At
    <i>k</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0 to 1.9&times; (the last compressive load), at
    <i>k</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;2/3 to 3.85&times; (shipped) and at
    <i>k</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;1, the overlap with the no-interaction null is
    97.1&nbsp;%, 97.9&nbsp;% and 99.0&nbsp;%; the participation ratio differs
    from that null by &minus;0.0003, +0.0012 and +0.0035; and the mean event
    size is 5.86, 5.83 and 5.90 against a largest of 845, 840 and 836. What
    moves is the active fraction &mdash; 51.9&nbsp;%, 52.8&nbsp;%,
    42.5&nbsp;% &mdash; and nothing else.
  </p>

  <h2>What is and is not being claimed</h2>

  <p>
    The machinery works and the numbers above are measured: 165,659 events over
    the load range shown, 52.8&nbsp;% of elements eventually slipping, a
    cumulative moment of 3.1&times;10<sup>21</sup>&nbsp;N&nbsp;m, and a largest
    single cascade of 840 elements. Every event reached equilibrium; none ran
    away, which is not automatic &mdash; the branching ratio averages 0.786 and
    its 90th percentile is 0.969, so this network sits just below criticality
    rather than comfortably away from it.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>Interaction does not choose which elements slip. It chooses how much
    each one slips.</b> That is the result, and it is not what we expected to
    find. The active set overlaps the <i>no-interaction null</i> &mdash; the
    set that would fail if elements could not talk to each other, selected by
    orientation and the regional load alone &mdash; by <b>97.9&nbsp;%</b>. Hold
    the weighting fixed and every geometric statistic of the two sets agrees:
    coherence differs by +0.0005, the participation ratio by
    +0.0012, the orientation fabric's leading eigenvalue by +0.0009, cluster
    planarity and linearity to three decimals. The faults that fail are the
    well-oriented ones, and elasticity barely moves that list.
  </p>

  <p>
    What elasticity does move is the <i>distribution of slip across that
    list</i>. Weighted by moment rather than by membership, the active set's
    participation ratio falls from 0.378 to 0.172 &mdash; moment is about 2.2
    times more concentrated than the footprint carrying it &mdash; and its
    coherence rises from 0.731 to 0.780. So slip piles onto a mutually
    better-aligned subset of the elements that were always going to fail. The
    network manufactures asperities out of slip amplitude, not out of which
    patches participate.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <b>Two of those numbers were wrong here first, in the same way.</b> The
    build originally weighted the active set by real slip while weighting every
    null by unit slip, then printed the difference in one column. That read as
    interaction concentrating the active set by a factor of two and sharpening
    its coherence by +0.057. Both effects were the weighting. Each statistic is
    now computed at matched weighting across every row, and the moment-weighted
    comparison is made against the same set's own unit-weighted value rather
    than across rows &mdash; which is how a factor of 2.2 in moment survived as
    real while the +0.057 became &minus;0.003.
  </div>

  <p>
    <b>The dimension question cannot be asked in this domain at all.</b> The
    box-counting window is bounded below by the <i>largest fault</i>, not the
    element size, because until a box exceeds a fault that fault reads locally
    two-dimensional. With a 37.9&nbsp;km fault in a 100&nbsp;km box the window
    spans <i>zero</i> decades, and the code refuses to report a slope rather
    than fitting one. Measuring <i>D</i><sub>active</sub> against
    <i>D</i><sub>geom</sub> needs either a domain of order 1,400&nbsp;km or a
    network whose largest fault is a small fraction of the box; the
    multiplicative-cascade configuration exists for exactly that and keeps its
    largest fault at 1.6&nbsp;% of the domain against 38&nbsp;% here.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two standing limits on what a dimension could ever say. The estimator is
    biased low by about 0.11 and that bias is <i>invisible to
    R<sup>2</sup></i> &mdash; every calibration fit reaches
    R<sup>2</sup>&nbsp;&ge;&nbsp;0.999, including one wrong by 0.14 &mdash; so
    only differences in a matched window are quoted. And a subset's dimension
    falls simply because it has fewer members: on a sheet that is exactly
    two-dimensional, a 5&nbsp;% random subset reads 0.36 lower and a 1&nbsp;%
    subset loses a full dimension. So &ldquo;the active set is
    lower-dimensional&rdquo; is nearly free, and the matched-cardinality null
    is the entire result rather than a formality.
  </p>

  <h2>Avalanches</h2>

  <p>
    An event here is one relaxation to equilibrium after a single element is
    pushed past its static strength &mdash; a cascade through the network, not
    a dynamic rupture, and nothing in it propagates in time. Sizes are broad:
    58.5&nbsp;% involve more than one element, the 99th percentile is 71
    elements and the largest is 840, against a median of 2.
  </p>

  <p>
    No exponent is quoted. A power law needs a defended lower cutoff, and these
    sizes are bounded above by the elements per fault &mdash; 38.6 on average
    here &mdash; so a fitted slope would be reporting the discretisation as
    much as the network. The survival function is given instead:
    <i>P</i>(&ge;2)&nbsp;=&nbsp;58.5&nbsp;%,
    <i>P</i>(&ge;5)&nbsp;=&nbsp;23.9&nbsp;%,
    <i>P</i>(&ge;10)&nbsp;=&nbsp;12.3&nbsp;%,
    <i>P</i>(&ge;50)&nbsp;=&nbsp;1.9&nbsp;%,
    <i>P</i>(&ge;100)&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.5&nbsp;%. An earlier configuration with
    small faults made this point the hard way: its event size was invariant to
    a 16&times; density change and a 25&times; change in the strength drop,
    because it was set by the discretisation within a fault rather than by the
    network &mdash; which is Rice's discreteness critique arriving uninvited.
  </p>

  <h2>The stress isosurface, and why this network has none yet</h2>

  <p>
    The viewer can raymarch a translucent isosurface of the elastic maximum
    shear stress &mdash; an invariant,
    (&sigma;<sub>1</sub>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;&sigma;<sub>3</sub>)/2, so it does
    not depend on a reference plane the way a Coulomb stress change would
    &mdash; with a threshold slider moving the shell. <b>The control is absent
    above because this network's volume has not been evaluated.</b> The page
    renders whatever the payload carries and omits the toggle when there is no
    volume, rather than offering a control that fails on click.
  </p>

  <p>
    <b>The reason is cost, and the cost was first estimated wrong by a factor
    of six.</b> Evaluating the field costs one kernel pair per grid point per
    element, and a stress readout parallelises at only 1.45&times; here &mdash;
    it is the <i>assembly</i> of an interaction matrix that parallelises at
    8.5&times;, a distinction worth keeping straight, because reading the wrong
    row is what produced the original estimate. Measured end to end at 54.1
    &micro;s/pair, a 24<sup>3</sup> grid against all 20,771 elements is about
    4.3 hours. The previous configuration's volume took two.
  </p>

  <p>
    A twenty-times-cheaper route was tried and rejected on measurement.
    Replacing each fault by its own rectangle carrying the same total moment
    &mdash; geometry exact, moment exact, only the slip distribution within a
    fault averaged &mdash; agrees with the exact field to a median of
    0.00&nbsp;% on a coarse grid, correlation 0.9997. But resolved against
    distance from the nearest fault it is wrong by a median 3.8&nbsp;% and a
    worst 38&nbsp;% within one fault length, falling below 0.1&nbsp;% only
    beyond about eight. An isosurface at a high threshold selects precisely the
    near-fault points, so the approximation is worst exactly where the picture
    is most interesting. Any volume published here is therefore exact over
    every element, and a coarse grid is the price: at 4.2&nbsp;km cells against
    2&ndash;38&nbsp;km faults it would show the network's <i>aggregate</i>
    stress structure, not a concentration at a fault tip.
  </p>

  <h2>Why there is a strength drop</h2>

  <p>
    With a single friction coefficient this experiment has no mechanism,
    because friction that does not weaken cannot go unstable. A patch unloads
    itself elastically at a stiffness <i>k</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;|<i>K<sub>ii</sub></i>|,
    and instability requires the strength to fall with slip faster than that
    &mdash; the classical criterion <i>k</i>&nbsp;&lt;&nbsp;|d&tau;/d&delta;|.
    Non-weakening Coulomb friction has d&tau;/d&delta;&nbsp;=&nbsp;0, so the
    inequality can never be satisfied at any patch size, stress or stiffness.
  </p>

  <p>
    The consequence is that the quasi-static solution is <b>continuous</b> in
    the load. Beyond failure, equilibrium pins the traction to the strength, so
    slip follows
    &delta;(&lambda;)&nbsp;=&nbsp;(&lambda;&thinsp;&tau;'&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;&tau;<sub>p</sub>)/|<i>K<sub>ii</sub></i>|
    &mdash; it grows from <i>zero slip</i> at a <i>finite</i> rate
    &tau;'/|<i>K<sub>ii</sub></i>|. Nothing jumps, so no event has a finite
    size and there is nothing to count. The first version of the solver
    demonstrated exactly that, advancing the load to the first failure and then
    relaxing zero slip indefinitely.
  </p>

  <p>
    So an element has a static strength
    &tau;<sub>p</sub>&nbsp;=&nbsp;&mu;<sub>s</sub>&sigma;'<sub>n</sub> it
    must reach to fail and a residual strength
    &tau;<sub>r</sub>&nbsp;=&nbsp;&mu;<sub>d</sub>&sigma;'<sub>n</sub> it
    relaxes down to. The drop
    &Delta;&tau;&nbsp;=&nbsp;&tau;<sub>p</sub>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;&tau;<sub>r</sub>
    forces a finite slip
    &Delta;&delta;&nbsp;=&nbsp;&Delta;&tau;/|<i>K<sub>ii</sub></i>| the instant
    an element fails, and that slip is what loads its neighbours and can
    trigger them in turn. This is slip-weakening in the limit of zero
    weakening distance, so the nucleation length collapses to zero and every
    element is unstable at any size &mdash; the price of not resolving a
    breakdown zone. Here
    &mu;<sub>s</sub>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.6 and
    &mu;<sub>d</sub>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.54 against 150&nbsp;MPa give
    90&nbsp;&rarr;&nbsp;81&nbsp;MPa, a <b>9&nbsp;MPa stress drop</b>, which sits
    inside the 1&ndash;10&nbsp;MPa range observed for real earthquakes. At a
    ratio of 1.0 there are no cascades by construction, and the code refuses
    that value rather than returning a null result that would look like a
    finding.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <b>It does not follow that the drop sets the avalanche statistics, and
    measurement says it does not.</b> A failing element slips
    &Delta;&tau;/|<i>K<sub>ii</sub></i>| and delivers
    <i>K<sub>ji</sub></i>&Delta;&tau;/|<i>K<sub>ii</sub></i>| to its neighbour,
    while the gap that neighbour must re-close after relocking is itself
    proportional to &Delta;&tau; &mdash; so the drop cancels from the
    triggering ratio, which is pure geometry. Measured: a <b>25&times; change
    in the drop moved mean avalanche size by under 2&nbsp;%</b>, with
    events&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;drop constant to 9&nbsp;%. What the drop does set
    is whether events exist at all, the moment carried by each, and how many
    there are &mdash; not how large they are in elements, which is most of what
    this page measures.
  </div>

  <h2>What the solver choice costs</h2>

  <p>
    A cascade has no unique end state &mdash; a triggered element lands anywhere
    between its dynamic and static strength depending on the order elements are
    visited &mdash; so there is no exact answer to validate against. What can be
    done is to vary the update rule and measure the spread. Measured on the
    smaller cascade configuration rather than on this network: between
    Gauss-Seidel and Jacobi, event counts move by 6.9&nbsp;% and mean event
    size by 4.3&nbsp;%; between element orderings, under 1&nbsp;%. But total
    slip is invariant to 0.1&nbsp;%, and the <b>active set came out bit
    identical</b> under every rule tried &mdash; symmetric difference zero. So
    the set every claim above is made on carries no solver dependence, while an
    avalanche exponent would carry that 7&nbsp;% as part of its uncertainty
    &mdash; one of several reasons none is quoted. The same sweep has not been
    repeated at 20,771 elements, where each relaxation is a hundred times more
    expensive.
  </p>

  <h2>One simplification worth naming</h2>

  <p>
    Effective normal stress is <b>uniform with depth</b> here, and that is a
    deliberate choice rather than an oversight. The real gradient is about
    0.0167&nbsp;GPa/km once hydrostatic pore pressure is removed, which across
    a 40&nbsp;km box is a factor of twenty in strength: the shallow elements
    would fail first, the active set would be a horizontal slab, and its
    measured dimension would be reporting the depth gradient rather than any
    mechanical localisation. Removing it leaves orientation and elastic
    interaction as the only things that can select which faults slip, which is
    the question. The depth-dependent case runs, and is important future work
    &mdash; it needs its own null, the same network with the gradient and the
    elastic interaction switched off, or depth and localisation stay confounded.
  </p>

  <p class="cap">
    Slip is stored quantised: log<sub>10</sub> into one byte per element per
    frame, 254 levels, zero reserved for &ldquo;has never slipped&rdquo; and
    drawn grey rather than at the bottom of the ramp, because a locked element
    is categorically different from one that slipped a little. The whole
    120&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;20,771 array is encoded in one pass, so the colour
    scale is global across frames &mdash; a per-frame scale would normalise away
    the growth being animated. The quantisation error is bounded by arithmetic
    rather than asserted, and the build measures what it wrote against that
    bound: 1.239&nbsp;% against a bound of 1.239&nbsp;%, a ratio of 1.000. The
    payload is 5.5&nbsp;MiB for 20,771 elements across 120 equilibria.
  </p>

